      "extract": "Before we engage in the non-Abelian Stokes theorem it seems reasonable to recall its Abelian version. The (Abelian) Stokes theorem says (see, e.g. \\[Spi65\\], for an excellent introduction to the subject) that we can convert an integral around a closed curve $C$ bounding some surface $S$ into an integral defined on this surface. Namely, in e.g. three dimensions $$\\oint_C \\vec{A} \\cdot d\\vec{s} =\\int_S {\\rm",
